const {H1,H2,H3,P,STORY,CASE,TIP,DIG,SEC,LN,PB,TBL}=require("./lib");
const c=[];

// ---------- EP 01 ----------
c.push(H1("Episode 01 — Judgment at Nuremberg: The Origin, Meaning and Evolution of Human Rights"));
c.push(H2("Covers: What 'human rights' means · The jurisprudential dimensions — natural law, positivism, social contract, utilitarian, Marxist, feminist and capability views · The evolution — Magna Carta to the Universal Declaration · Nuremberg and the birth of the modern law · Characteristics of human rights"));
c.push(STORY("Nuremberg, 20 November 1945. In Courtroom 600 of the Palace of Justice, twenty-one leaders of the Third Reich sat in the dock before four judges from the United States, Britain, France and the Soviet Union. The London Charter of August 1945 had given the International Military Tribunal jurisdiction over three crimes — crimes against peace, war crimes, and a new one, crimes against humanity: 'murder, extermination, enslavement, deportation, and other inhumane acts committed against any civilian population, before or during the war, or persecutions on political, racial or religious grounds'. The defence made the argument that had always been unanswerable in international law: what a State did to its own citizens, inside its own borders, under its own laws, was its own affair; the Nuremberg Laws of 1935 were German law; the defendants had obeyed orders under a sovereign; and there was no law under which to try them — nullum crimen sine lege. The Tribunal's answer, delivered on 1 October 1946, was that individuals have international duties which transcend the national obligations of obedience imposed by the individual State; that crimes against international law are committed by men, not by abstract entities; that superior orders are no defence; and that the principle of legality is a principle of justice which cannot protect those who knew their acts were wrong. Twelve were sentenced to death. Within three years the General Assembly had affirmed the 'Nuremberg principles', adopted the Genocide Convention, and proclaimed the Universal Declaration of Human Rights. The trial is the point at which the individual became a subject of international law, and sovereignty stopped being an answer."));
c.push(H3("Meaning of human rights"));
c.push(P("**Human rights** are the rights that belong to every human being by virtue of being human — not granted by the State, not earned, not dependent on citizenship, status, conduct or merit, and not capable of being taken away, though they may be limited. The **Protection of Human Rights Act 1993, Sec 2(1)(d)** (India's statutory definition): 'human rights' means the rights relating to life, liberty, equality and dignity of the individual guaranteed by the Constitution or embodied in the International Covenants and enforceable by courts in India. The UN's definition (the Vienna Declaration 1993, para 1): human rights and fundamental freedoms are the birthright of all human beings; their protection and promotion is the first responsibility of Governments. The older vocabulary — 'natural rights' (Locke), 'rights of man' (Paine, the French Declaration of 1789), 'fundamental rights' (the Constitution's Part III), 'civil liberties' — points to the same idea from different angles: natural rights emphasise the source (nature or God), rights of man the holder, fundamental rights the constitutional status, human rights the universality. **Characteristics** (from the Vienna Declaration and the literature): (1) **universal** — for everyone, everywhere; (2) **inherent** — they arise from human dignity, not from grant; (3) **inalienable** — they cannot be surrendered or taken away, though their exercise may be restricted; (4) **indivisible and interdependent** — civil, political, economic, social and cultural rights are of equal importance and each depends on the others (Vienna para 5: 'all human rights are universal, indivisible and interdependent and interrelated'); (5) **equal and non-discriminatory** — Art 2 UDHR; (6) they impose **duties** on States — to respect (not to interfere), to protect (against third parties) and to fulfil (to take positive measures) — the tripartite typology of Shue and the Maastricht Guidelines; (7) they are **dynamic** — new rights are recognised (the environment, water, the internet, a healthy environment — the General Assembly's 2022 resolution); and (8) they carry **correlative responsibilities** of the individual (Art 29 UDHR; the African Charter's duties)."));
c.push(H3("Jurisprudential dimensions"));
c.push(P("**(1) Natural law** — the oldest foundation: rights exist by nature, discoverable by reason, prior to and higher than the State's law. The Stoics (a universal law of reason binding all humans), Cicero, the Christian natural law of Aquinas (lex naturalis participating in eternal law), the Spanish scholastics Vitoria and Suárez (the rights of the Indians of the Americas against the conquistadors — the first 'human rights' argument in international law), Grotius (natural law would exist 'even if God did not exist'), and the seventeenth-century social-contract theorists. **Locke** (Two Treatises, 1689): men in the state of nature have natural rights to life, liberty and property; government is created by consent to protect them and may be overthrown when it violates them — the theory behind the English Bill of Rights (1689), the American Declaration of Independence (1776: 'endowed by their Creator with certain unalienable Rights... Life, Liberty and the pursuit of Happiness') and the French Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen (1789: 'Men are born and remain free and equal in rights'). **Rousseau** — the general will; **Kant** — human dignity as an end in itself, the categorical imperative, the cosmopolitan right. The revival of natural law after 1945 (Radbruch's formula — a law that is intolerably unjust is not law; Nuremberg; Finnis's Natural Law and Natural Rights, 1980) is the direct answer to legal positivism's inability to condemn Nazi law. **(2) Positivism** — rights exist only as posited by law; Bentham called natural rights 'nonsense upon stilts' and imprescriptible rights 'nonsense upon stilts'; Austin — law is the command of the sovereign; rights are creations of the legal system. The positivist contribution to human rights is paradoxically the largest: the treaties, the covenants, the constitutions are positive law, and the movement's strategy has been to convert moral claims into enforceable rules (Hart's later concession that there is a 'minimum content of natural law'). **(3) Utilitarianism** — Bentham and Mill: rights are justified by their tendency to promote the greatest happiness; Mill's harm principle (On Liberty, 1859) as the basis of civil liberties; the criticism (Dworkin: rights as 'trumps' over utilitarian calculations of the collective good) that utility can justify sacrificing the individual. **(4) The historical and sociological schools** — Savigny (rights as products of the Volksgeist), Maine, Duguit (social solidarity), Pound (interests) — explain rights as evolving social claims. **(5) Marxism** — Marx's 'On the Jewish Question' (1843): the rights of man are the rights of egoistic bourgeois man separated from the community; real emancipation is economic; the Soviet constitutions emphasised social and economic rights and collective rights; the Cold War split of the Covenants (Episode 04) is this debate institutionalised. **(6) Rawls** — A Theory of Justice (1971): the two principles chosen behind the veil of ignorance — equal basic liberties, and the difference principle; The Law of Peoples (1999) — a minimal list of 'human rights proper' as the standard for international toleration. **(7) Dworkin** — rights as trumps; Taking Rights Seriously (1977); the right to equal concern and respect as the foundational right. **(8) Sen and Nussbaum** — the **capabilities approach**: human rights are claims to the substantive freedoms (capabilities) people need to lead lives they have reason to value; Sen's Development as Freedom (1999), Nussbaum's list of ten central capabilities — the basis of the Human Development Index and the Indian Supreme Court's expansive reading of Art 21. **(9) Feminist jurisprudence** — MacKinnon, Charlesworth and Chinkin: the public/private divide in human rights law leaves violence against women in the 'private' sphere unaddressed; CEDAW and the 1993 Vienna recognition that 'women's rights are human rights'. **(10) Cultural relativism vs universalism** — the 'Asian values' debate of the 1990s (the Bangkok Declaration 1993), the Vienna Declaration's answer (universality 'beyond question' while national and regional particularities are borne in mind), and the responses of Donnelly ('relative universality'), An-Na'im (cross-cultural legitimacy) and Sen (the argumentative Indian). **(11) The Indian tradition** — dharma as duty-based order, the Buddhist and Jain ethics of ahimsa, Ashoka's edicts of tolerance, Kautilya's raja-dharma, the Bhakti and Sufi egalitarianism, the reformers (Ram Mohan Roy, Phule, Ambedkar — 'Annihilation of Caste' as a human-rights text), Gandhi's insistence that rights flow from duties (his 1947 letter to UNESCO), and the Constitution's synthesis of liberty (Part III), social justice (Part IV) and fraternity (the Preamble)."));
c.push(H3("Evolution — the milestones"));
c.push(TBL([
["Year","Document / event","Contribution"],
["c. 539 BC","The Cyrus Cylinder","Religious freedom and the release of slaves — claimed as the first charter (contested)"],
["1215","Magna Carta (England)","No free man to be imprisoned or dispossessed except by lawful judgment of his peers or the law of the land (cl. 39); the origin of due process and habeas corpus"],
["1628 / 1679 / 1689","Petition of Right; Habeas Corpus Act; Bill of Rights (England)","No taxation without Parliament; no arbitrary imprisonment; no cruel and unusual punishment; free elections and speech in Parliament"],
["1776","Virginia Declaration of Rights; US Declaration of Independence","Inherent rights of all men; government by consent; the right of revolution"],
["1789 / 1791","French Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen; US Bill of Rights","Liberty, property, security, resistance to oppression; equality before the law; the first ten amendments — speech, religion, arms, search, due process, jury"],
["1807-1888","Abolition of the slave trade (Britain 1807) and slavery (Britain 1833, US 1865, Brazil 1888)","The first international human-rights campaign; the 1926 Slavery Convention"],
["1864","First Geneva Convention (Dunant, ICRC)","Humanitarian law — the wounded in war"],
["1919","League of Nations Covenant; ILO; minorities treaties; mandates","International labour standards; protection of minorities in the new States; the 'sacred trust' for mandated peoples"],
["1941","Roosevelt's Four Freedoms; the Atlantic Charter","Freedom of speech and worship, freedom from want and fear — the wartime programme"],
["1945","UN Charter; the London Charter; Nuremberg","'Faith in fundamental human rights' (Preamble); Arts 1(3), 55, 56; crimes against humanity; individual responsibility"],
["1948","Genocide Convention (9 Dec); Universal Declaration (10 Dec); American Declaration; ILO Convention 87","The International Bill of Rights begins"],
["1950 / 1966","European Convention; the two Covenants","Regional enforcement; binding universal treaties"],
["1965-2006","CERD, CEDAW, CAT, CRC, ICRMW, CRPD, CED","The specialised conventions"],
["1993","Vienna Declaration; OHCHR","Universality, indivisibility; the High Commissioner"],
["1998 / 2002","Rome Statute; ICC","Permanent international criminal court"],
["2005 / 2006","Responsibility to Protect; Human Rights Council","Sovereignty as responsibility; the UPR"]
],[1500,3400,4100]));
c.push(H3("Nuremberg and the birth of the modern law"));
c.push(P("Before 1945 international law was a law between States; the individual was an 'object', protected — if at all — through his own State (diplomatic protection), and the way a State treated its own nationals was within its domestic jurisdiction. The exceptions were partial: the abolition of the slave trade, the humanitarian law of war (Geneva 1864, Hague 1899 and 1907), the minorities treaties of 1919 (enforceable by petition to the League Council — the Upper Silesia cases at the Permanent Court), the ILO conventions, and the mandates system. The Holocaust destroyed the premise. The **London Charter (8 August 1945)** defined crimes against humanity; the **Nuremberg Tribunal** (1945-46) and the **Tokyo Tribunal** (1946-48) applied it; the **General Assembly's Resolution 95(I) (1946)** affirmed the 'principles of international law recognised by the Charter of the Nuremberg Tribunal and the judgment of the Tribunal', later formulated by the International Law Commission (1950) as the seven Nuremberg Principles (individual responsibility; internal law no defence; head-of-State immunity no defence; superior orders no defence; the right to a fair trial; the three crimes; complicity). The **UN Charter (26 June 1945)** made human rights a purpose of the Organisation (Art 1(3)) and a pledge of the members (Arts 55-56); the **Genocide Convention (9 December 1948)** made genocide an international crime in peace or war; the **Universal Declaration (10 December 1948)** stated the rights; and the Geneva Conventions of 1949 rewrote the law of war around the protection of persons. The doctrinal consequences: the individual is a subject of international law with rights (the Covenants, the petition procedures) and duties (international crimes); a State's treatment of its own nationals is a matter of international concern, not domestic jurisdiction alone (Art 2(7) is read subject to Chapter VII and the human-rights obligations); and certain norms are **jus cogens** (the prohibitions of genocide, slavery, torture, racial discrimination — Art 53 of the Vienna Convention on the Law of Treaties; the ICJ in Belgium v. Senegal, 2012) with obligations **erga omnes** (Barcelona Traction, 1970 — 'the principles and rules concerning the basic rights of the human person')."));
c.push(CASE("The Nuremberg Judgment (1946) 41 AJIL 172","Crimes against international law are committed by men; individuals have international duties transcending national obedience; superior orders are no defence; the principle of legality is a principle of justice, not a shield for those who knew their acts were criminal."));
c.push(CASE("Barcelona Traction (Belgium v. Spain), ICJ 1970","Obligations erga omnes — owed to the international community as a whole — include those derived from the outlawing of aggression and genocide and from 'the principles and rules concerning the basic rights of the human person, including protection from slavery and racial discrimination'."));
c.push(CASE("Filartiga v. Pena-Irala, 630 F.2d 876 (2d Cir. 1980)","The prohibition of official torture is part of customary international law, so that a Paraguayan torturer could be sued in a US court under the Alien Tort Statute — 'the torturer has become, like the pirate and slave trader before him, hostis humani generis'."));
c.push(CASE("Questions relating to the Obligation to Prosecute or Extradite (Belgium v. Senegal), ICJ 2012","The prohibition of torture is jus cogens; the Torture Convention's obligation to prosecute or extradite (Hissène Habré) is owed erga omnes partes, so that any State party may invoke it."));
c.push(DIG("The Nuremberg Principles live on in the Rome Statute (Art 25, 27, 33); the UN's digital archives of the trial (the Harvard Nuremberg Trials Project) and the OHCHR's Universal Human Rights Index put the sources online; and the 'right to internet access' and 'digital rights' (the 2016 Human Rights Council resolution that the same rights people have offline must be protected online) are the newest chapter of the evolution."));
c.push(TIP("Part A: 'Define human rights', 'Characteristics of human rights', 'Natural rights theory', 'Nuremberg principles', 'Magna Carta', 'Jus cogens', 'Obligations erga omnes', 'Capabilities approach'. Part B: 'Explain the meaning of human rights and discuss the jurisprudential foundations — natural law, positivism, utilitarianism, Marxism and the modern theories' — the definitions, the eleven dimensions with one thinker each; or 'Trace the evolution of the concept of human rights from Magna Carta to the Universal Declaration' — the table with Nuremberg as the turning point. Part C: a State argues that its treatment of its own citizens is a domestic matter — Nuremberg, Art 55-56 UN Charter, Barcelona Traction; a soldier pleads superior orders for killing prisoners — Nuremberg Principle IV, Art 33 Rome Statute (orders to commit genocide or crimes against humanity are manifestly unlawful); a Government says human rights are a Western concept — Vienna 1993 universality, the Indian tradition, Sen."));
c.push(PB());

// ---------- EP 02 ----------
c.push(H1("Episode 02 — Rwanda, 1994: The Three Generations, Sovereignty and the United Nations"));
c.push(H2("Covers: First, second and third generation rights (Vasak) · Sovereignty vs human rights — domestic jurisdiction (Art 2(7)), humanitarian intervention, the Responsibility to Protect · Human rights and the United Nations — the Charter provisions (Preamble, Arts 1(3), 13, 55, 56, 62, 68, 76) and the organs (General Assembly, Security Council, ECOSOC, Trusteeship Council, ICJ, Secretariat) · Promotion and protection — the standard-setting, monitoring and enforcement functions"));
c.push(STORY("Kigali, April 1994. On 6 April the aircraft carrying the Presidents of Rwanda and Burundi was shot down over Kigali airport. Within hours, roadblocks went up and the killing began: the Interahamwe militia and the army, with lists prepared in advance, began to murder Tutsi and moderate Hutu, house by house, with machetes and clubs, while Radio Mille Collines broadcast the names of those to be found. The United Nations had 2,500 peacekeepers in the country under General Roméo Dallaire, who had warned New York in January of the plan and been told not to act; on 21 April the Security Council reduced the force to 270. In a hundred days about 800,000 people were killed — the fastest genocide in recorded history — while the Council debated whether to use the word 'genocide', because the word carried obligations under the 1948 Convention. The Rwandan Patriotic Front ended the killing in July; the Council created the International Criminal Tribunal for Rwanda in November; the Tribunal's Akayesu judgment (1998) was the first conviction for genocide by an international court, and the first to hold that rape can be an act of genocide. The failure changed the doctrine. Kofi Annan asked the General Assembly in 1999 whether sovereignty could be a shield for mass atrocity; the Canadian-sponsored International Commission on Intervention and State Sovereignty answered in 2001 with the 'Responsibility to Protect'; and the 2005 World Summit's Outcome Document (paras 138-139) made it the UN's position: each State has the responsibility to protect its population from genocide, war crimes, ethnic cleansing and crimes against humanity; the international community should help; and when a State manifestly fails, the Security Council may act under Chapter VII. Rwanda is the case that stands at the intersection of every theme of this episode: the generations of rights, the limits of sovereignty, and the machinery of the United Nations."));
c.push(H3("The three generations"));
c.push(P("The Czech-French jurist **Karel Vasak** (1977, at the International Institute of Human Rights, Strasbourg) borrowed the French Revolution's slogan to classify rights by their historical emergence and their character. **First generation — liberté — civil and political rights**: the rights of the eighteenth-century revolutions — life, liberty and security of the person; freedom from torture, slavery and arbitrary arrest; fair trial; privacy; freedom of movement, thought, conscience, religion, expression, assembly and association; participation in government, the vote; equality before the law. They are 'negative' rights (the State must refrain), immediately enforceable, justiciable, and the subject of the ICCPR (1966) and the Constitution's Part III. **Second generation — égalité — economic, social and cultural rights**: the rights of the nineteenth- and twentieth-century labour and socialist movements and the welfare State — work and just conditions, trade unions, social security, an adequate standard of living (food, clothing, housing), health, education, participation in cultural life; the Mexican Constitution of 1917, the Weimar Constitution of 1919, the Soviet constitutions, the ILO; 'positive' rights requiring State action and resources, subject to 'progressive realisation' (Art 2(1) ICESCR), traditionally regarded as non-justiciable programmes — the Constitution's Part IV — though the Indian Supreme Court's reading of Art 21 (Olga Tellis, Paschim Banga, Unni Krishnan, PUCL's food case), the South African Constitutional Court (Grootboom, Treatment Action Campaign) and the 2008 Optional Protocol to the ICESCR have made them increasingly justiciable. **Third generation — fraternité — solidarity or collective rights**: the rights that emerged with decolonisation and the environmental movement, held by peoples and communities rather than individuals, and requiring international cooperation — the right to self-determination (common Art 1 of the Covenants), to development (the 1986 Declaration on the Right to Development), to peace (the 1984 Declaration), to a healthy environment (Stockholm 1972, Rio 1992, the 2022 General Assembly resolution), to the common heritage of mankind (the Law of the Sea Convention's deep seabed; the Moon Treaty), to humanitarian assistance, to communication, and the rights of minorities and indigenous peoples; the African Charter (1981) is the treaty that most fully embodies them ('peoples' rights' in Arts 19-24). **Critique**: the generations are not sequential (the ILO's social rights predate many civil rights; the UDHR contains all three), not hierarchical (Vienna 1993: indivisible and interdependent), and the 'negative/positive' distinction is false — a fair trial needs courts (positive), and the right to food includes freedom from State interference with one's livelihood (negative); Shue's tripartite duties (respect, protect, fulfil) apply to every right. Some writers add a **fourth generation** (the rights of future generations, genetic and digital rights). The classification survives as a teaching device and as the explanation of the two Covenants."));
c.push(H3("Sovereignty vs human rights"));
c.push(P("**Sovereignty** — Bodin (1576) and Hobbes: the supreme authority within a territory, and in international law (Westphalia 1648; the Island of Palmas award, 1928) the independence of States from external control — has three legal expressions in the UN Charter: the **sovereign equality** of members (Art 2(1)); the prohibition of the **use of force** against territorial integrity or political independence (Art 2(4)); and **domestic jurisdiction** — **Art 2(7)**: 'Nothing contained in the present Charter shall authorise the United Nations to intervene in matters which are essentially within the domestic jurisdiction of any State or shall require the Members to submit such matters to settlement under the present Charter; but this principle shall not prejudice the application of enforcement measures under Chapter VII.' The human-rights answer developed in four stages. **(1) The matter is not 'essentially domestic'** — from the General Assembly's first years (South Africa's treatment of Indians, 1946; apartheid from 1952; the ICJ's Namibia opinion, 1971), the practice established that a State's human-rights record is a legitimate subject of UN discussion, resolutions and investigation (the 1235 and 1503 procedures of 1967 and 1970), because the members have pledged themselves under Arts 55-56 and because gross violations threaten peace. **(2) Chapter VII** — the Security Council may determine that a humanitarian catastrophe is a threat to international peace and security (Art 39) and authorise measures: sanctions against Rhodesia (1966) and South Africa (1977, the arms embargo), the no-fly zones and 'safe havens' in Iraq (Resolution 688, 1991), Somalia (794, 1992 — the first purely humanitarian Chapter VII intervention), Bosnia, Haiti (940, 1994), Rwanda (929, 1994 — Opération Turquoise, too late), East Timor (1264, 1999), Libya (1973, 2011 — the first invocation of R2P to authorise force). **(3) Humanitarian intervention without Council authorisation** — the unilateral doctrine: India in East Pakistan (1971, which India first justified on humanitarian grounds and then on self-defence), Tanzania in Uganda (1979), Vietnam in Cambodia (1978), NATO in Kosovo (1999 — 'illegal but legitimate', the Independent International Commission); the doctrine is contested as a breach of Art 2(4) (Nicaragua v. United States, 1986 — the ICJ rejected the use of force as a method of ensuring respect for human rights) and as open to abuse (Russia's Georgia 2008 and Ukraine 2022 justifications). **(4) The Responsibility to Protect (R2P)** — the ICISS report (2001) reframed sovereignty as responsibility: the primary responsibility of the State to protect its people, the international community's responsibility to prevent, to react and to rebuild, and force as a last resort under 'just cause', 'right intention', 'proportional means', 'reasonable prospects' and 'right authority' (the Council); the **2005 World Summit Outcome, paras 138-139**, adopted the first two pillars and limited the third to Council action under Chapters VI and VII 'on a case-by-case basis'; the Secretary-General's 2009 report set out the three pillars; Libya (2011) was its first and, after the regime change, most contested application; Syria (2011-) its most conspicuous failure (Russian and Chinese vetoes). The current law: sovereignty is no shield against international scrutiny, reporting, censure and sanctions; forcible intervention requires Council authorisation; and the Council's failures (Rwanda, Srebrenica, Syria, Myanmar) are political, not legal, limits. India's position — traditionally strong on Art 2(7) and non-intervention (its objections to the Kosovo bombing and to R2P's third pillar; its abstention on Libya), while accepting the first two pillars and the UN's human-rights machinery."));
c.push(H3("Human rights and the UN Charter"));
c.push(P("The Charter mentions human rights seven times, the result of the pressure of NGOs and the smaller States at San Francisco (1945) against the great powers' draft (Dumbarton Oaks, 1944) which had mentioned them once. **Preamble**: 'We the peoples of the United Nations determined... to reaffirm faith in fundamental human rights, in the dignity and worth of the human person, in the equal rights of men and women and of nations large and small'. **Art 1(3)** — a purpose of the UN: 'to achieve international co-operation in solving international problems of an economic, social, cultural, or humanitarian character, and in promoting and encouraging respect for human rights and for fundamental freedoms for all without distinction as to race, sex, language, or religion'. **Art 13(1)(b)** — the General Assembly shall initiate studies and make recommendations for 'assisting in the realisation of human rights and fundamental freedoms for all'. **Art 55** — 'With a view to the creation of conditions of stability and well-being... the United Nations shall promote: (a) higher standards of living, full employment, and conditions of economic and social progress and development; (b) solutions of international economic, social, health, and related problems; and international cultural and educational co-operation; and (c) universal respect for, and observance of, human rights and fundamental freedoms for all without distinction as to race, sex, language, or religion.' **Art 56** — 'All Members pledge themselves to take joint and separate action in co-operation with the Organisation for the achievement of the purposes set forth in Article 55' — the legal obligation (the ICJ in Namibia, 1971: South Africa's apartheid in the mandated territory was 'a flagrant violation of the purposes and principles of the Charter'). **Art 62(2)** — ECOSOC may make recommendations for the purpose of promoting respect for and observance of human rights; **Art 68** — ECOSOC 'shall set up commissions in economic and social fields and for the promotion of human rights' — the only commission the Charter names, created as the Commission on Human Rights in 1946. **Art 76(c)** — a basic objective of the trusteeship system: to encourage respect for human rights. The Charter does not define the rights (the UDHR was to do that) and the argument whether Arts 55-56 impose binding obligations was settled by practice and by the ICJ (Namibia; the Tehran Hostages case, 1980 — 'wrongfully to deprive human beings of their freedom... is manifestly incompatible with the principles of the Charter'). The **organs**: (1) the **General Assembly** — resolutions (the UDHR, the Declarations, the conventions adopted for signature), the Third Committee (social, humanitarian and cultural), the election of the Council's members, the Special Sessions; (2) the **Security Council** — Chapter VII action, the ad hoc tribunals (ICTY 1993, ICTR 1994 — created under Chapter VII), referrals to the ICC (Darfur 2005, Libya 2011), sanctions committees, the 'Women, Peace and Security' (1325, 2000) and 'Children and Armed Conflict' agendas, the Arria-formula meetings; (3) **ECOSOC** — the Commission on Human Rights (1946-2006, replaced by the Human Rights Council, a subsidiary of the General Assembly), the Commission on the Status of Women (1946), the Permanent Forum on Indigenous Issues (2000), consultative status for NGOs (Art 71); (4) the **Trusteeship Council** — suspended in 1994 after Palau's independence; (5) the **International Court of Justice** — contentious cases and advisory opinions (Episode 11); (6) the **Secretariat** — the Secretary-General's good offices and reports, and the **Office of the High Commissioner for Human Rights** (1993) in Geneva; plus the specialised agencies (ILO, UNESCO, WHO, FAO), the funds and programmes (UNICEF, UNDP, UNHCR, UN Women, UNFPA), and the treaty bodies (Episode 07)."));
c.push(H3("Promotion and protection"));
c.push(P("The UN's human-rights work is conventionally divided into **promotion** (standard-setting, education, technical assistance, the advancement of rights) and **protection** (monitoring, investigation, condemnation, remedies). **Standard-setting**: the Declarations (UDHR 1948; the Rights of the Child 1959; the Elimination of Racial Discrimination 1963; Women 1967; Torture 1975; Development 1986; Minorities 1992; Human Rights Defenders 1998; Indigenous Peoples 2007) and the treaties (the nine core conventions with their optional protocols — Episode 07), drafted in the Commission/Council and adopted by the General Assembly. **Monitoring**: the treaty bodies' periodic reports, the Council's Universal Periodic Review (every State every four and a half years), the Special Procedures (thematic and country rapporteurs, working groups, independent experts — about 60 mandates), the High Commissioner's reports and country offices, the commissions of inquiry (Syria, North Korea, Myanmar, Gaza). **Complaints**: the individual communications under the optional protocols, the Council's confidential complaint procedure (the successor of 1503), the ILO's freedom-of-association committee. **Enforcement**: the Council's resolutions and special sessions, the General Assembly's condemnations, the Security Council's Chapter VII measures, the international criminal tribunals, the ICJ (the Genocide Convention cases), and 'naming and shaming' through reports — the weakest link in the system, where the machinery of States judging States meets the politics of the Council (the election of violators to the Human Rights Council; the vetoes). **Technical cooperation** (the OHCHR's advisory services, the national human-rights institutions under the Paris Principles) and **education** (the 2011 Declaration on Human Rights Education and Training) are the promotion side. The **Vienna World Conference (1993)** — with its Declaration and Programme of Action — set the modern agenda: universality and indivisibility, the High Commissioner, national institutions, women's rights as human rights, the rights of the child, the elimination of racism, the right to development; and the **Millennium Declaration (2000)** and the **Sustainable Development Goals (2015)** are the development-rights agenda."));
c.push(CASE("Prosecutor v. Akayesu, ICTR 1998","The first conviction for genocide by an international tribunal; rape and sexual violence can constitute genocide when committed with intent to destroy a group; the definition of the protected 'ethnic group' by the perpetrators' perception."));
c.push(CASE("Legal Consequences for States of the Continued Presence of South Africa in Namibia, ICJ Advisory Opinion 1971","Apartheid in the mandated territory violated the Charter's human-rights purposes and principles; Arts 55-56 are legal obligations; the Assembly could terminate the mandate."));
c.push(CASE("Military and Paramilitary Activities in and against Nicaragua (Nicaragua v. United States), ICJ 1986","The use of force cannot be justified as a method of ensuring respect for human rights; the protection of human rights is not a ground for unilateral intervention; Art 2(4) is customary law."));
c.push(CASE("Bosnia and Herzegovina v. Serbia and Montenegro, ICJ 2007","Srebrenica was genocide; Serbia had violated its obligation to prevent genocide (the duty of due diligence under Art I of the Convention) and to punish it by not surrendering Mladić; the duty to prevent arises when a State learns of a serious risk."));
c.push(DIG("The Council's Universal Periodic Review is conducted and archived online (the UPR-Info database); the Security Council's atrocity-prevention framework and the Secretary-General's 'Framework of Analysis for Atrocity Crimes' (2014) use open-source and satellite data; the ICTR's archive is in Arusha and online; and the 'Radio Mille Collines' lesson — incitement through media — is the origin of Art 20 ICCPR's application to broadcasting and the Rome Statute's 'direct and public incitement' offence."));
c.push(TIP("Part A: 'Three generations of human rights', 'Third generation rights', 'Art 2(7) UN Charter', 'Humanitarian intervention', 'Responsibility to Protect', 'Art 55 and 56', 'Commission on Human Rights', 'Vienna Declaration 1993'. Part B: 'Explain the three generations of human rights and the criticism of the classification'; or 'Sovereignty versus human rights — discuss the tension with reference to Art 2(7), humanitarian intervention and the Responsibility to Protect'; or 'Discuss the provisions of the UN Charter on human rights and the role of the principal organs in their promotion and protection'. Part C: a State massacres a minority and invokes Art 2(7) against a General Assembly resolution — not 'essentially domestic' (South Africa practice, Namibia), Arts 55-56; a neighbouring State intervenes militarily to stop the massacre without Council authorisation — Art 2(4), Nicaragua; legitimacy debate (Kosovo), R2P requires Council authority; the Council is vetoed — the 'Uniting for Peace' resolution (377(V), 1950) lets the Assembly recommend, and the 2022 veto-initiative resolution requires a debate; a State denies that the right to food is a 'right' — Vienna indivisibility, ICESCR Art 11, PUCL v. Union of India."));
c.push(PB());

// ---------- EP 03 ----------
c.push(H1("Episode 03 — All Human Beings: Hansa Mehta and the Universal Declaration of Human Rights"));
c.push(H2("Covers: The drafting — the Commission on Human Rights, the Drafting Committee, the personalities · The structure and the thirty articles · The legal status — a declaration, not a treaty; customary law; the 'International Bill of Rights' · The influence on constitutions, including India's · The Proclamation of Tehran (1968) and the Vienna Declaration (1993) · Criticisms and the relativist debate"));
c.push(STORY("Lake Success, New York, 1947-48. The Commission on Human Rights, chaired by Eleanor Roosevelt, had eighteen members and a drafting committee of eight; the first draft was written by the Canadian John Humphrey, the Secretariat's director of human rights, from four hundred pages of constitutions and proposals; the Frenchman René Cassin turned it into a structured text; the Lebanese philosopher Charles Malik and the Chinese Confucian scholar Peng-chun Chang argued over its foundations — natural law or a plural ethic — and agreed to leave God out; the Soviet delegate insisted on economic and social rights; the Latin Americans brought the Bogotá Declaration of the Rights and Duties of Man. India's representative was Hansa Mehta — a Gujarati educationist, a member of the Constituent Assembly, the first woman Vice-Chancellor of an Indian university. When the draft of Article 1 read 'All men are born free and equal', she objected: in most of the world 'men' would be read to mean men, and the Declaration would be used to deny women what it promised. Eleanor Roosevelt thought 'men' was understood generically; Mehta, supported by the Danish delegate Bodil Begtrup and the Dominican Minerva Bernardino, persisted. The final text reads 'All human beings are born free and equal in dignity and rights.' The Declaration was adopted by the General Assembly in Paris on 10 December 1948 by forty-eight votes to none, with eight abstentions — the Soviet bloc, Saudi Arabia and South Africa. Cassin called it a portico: a foundation (dignity, liberty, equality, fraternity — Arts 1-2), four columns (the personal rights, Arts 3-11; the rights of the individual in civil society, Arts 12-17; the political rights, Arts 18-21; the economic, social and cultural rights, Arts 22-27), and a pediment (the duties and limits, Arts 28-30). It is the most translated document in the world, and Hansa Mehta's phrase is its first sentence."));
c.push(H3("The drafting"));
c.push(P("**The Commission on Human Rights** was set up by ECOSOC under Art 68 in 1946 (the 'nuclear' commission of nine, then eighteen members) and asked to prepare an international bill of rights; it decided on three parts — a declaration, a convention and measures of implementation — the last two becoming the Covenants of 1966. **The Drafting Committee** (1947): Eleanor Roosevelt (United States, chair), Peng-chun Chang (China, vice-chair), Charles Malik (Lebanon, rapporteur), René Cassin (France), Hernán Santa Cruz (Chile), Alexander Bogomolov (USSR), Geoffrey Wilson (UK), William Hodgson (Australia), with **John Humphrey** (Canada, Secretariat) as the author of the first 48-article outline drawn from the constitutions of the world and the proposals of the American Law Institute, the Inter-American Juridical Committee and NGOs; **Hansa Mehta** (India), Bodil Begtrup (Denmark, chair of the Commission on the Status of Women), Minerva Bernardino (Dominican Republic) and Lakshmi Menon (India, in the Third Committee) shaped the gender-neutral language and the non-discrimination clause; **Cassin's** redraft gave the structure and the preamble; the Commission's second and third sessions (December 1947, June 1948), the Third Committee's 81 meetings in the autumn of 1948 (with over 160 amendments, Malik chairing), and the plenary of 10 December 1948 completed it. The abstentions: the six Soviet-bloc States (the Declaration was insufficiently attentive to economic rights and to the State's role, and lacked a condemnation of fascism), Saudi Arabia (Art 16's equal marriage rights and Art 18's right to change religion), South Africa (the whole, given apartheid). Honduras and Yemen were absent."));
c.push(H3("Structure and the thirty articles"));
c.push(TBL([
["Article","Right"],
["Preamble","Inherent dignity and equal and inalienable rights as the foundation of freedom, justice and peace; barbarous acts; the four freedoms; the Charter pledge; 'a common standard of achievement for all peoples and all nations'"],
["1","All human beings are born free and equal in dignity and rights; reason and conscience; the spirit of brotherhood"],
["2","Entitlement to all rights without distinction of any kind — race, colour, sex, language, religion, political or other opinion, national or social origin, property, birth or other status; no distinction on the basis of the status of the territory"],
["3","Life, liberty and security of person"],
["4","No slavery or servitude; the slave trade prohibited"],
["5","No torture or cruel, inhuman or degrading treatment or punishment"],
["6","Recognition everywhere as a person before the law"],
["7","Equality before the law and equal protection without discrimination"],
["8","Effective remedy by national tribunals for violations of fundamental rights"],
["9","No arbitrary arrest, detention or exile"],
["10","Fair and public hearing by an independent and impartial tribunal"],
["11","Presumption of innocence; no retroactive penal law"],
["12","No arbitrary interference with privacy, family, home or correspondence; protection of honour and reputation"],
["13","Freedom of movement and residence within the State; the right to leave any country and return"],
["14","The right to seek and enjoy asylum from persecution (not for non-political crimes)"],
["15","The right to a nationality; no arbitrary deprivation"],
["16","Marriage and family — equal rights of men and women; free and full consent; the family as the natural and fundamental group unit"],
["17","Property — alone or in association; no arbitrary deprivation"],
["18","Freedom of thought, conscience and religion, including to change religion and to manifest it"],
["19","Freedom of opinion and expression, including to seek, receive and impart information through any media regardless of frontiers"],
["20","Peaceful assembly and association; no compulsion to belong to an association"],
["21","Participation in government directly or through representatives; equal access to public service; the will of the people expressed in periodic and genuine elections by universal and equal suffrage and secret vote"],
["22","Social security; economic, social and cultural rights indispensable for dignity and the free development of personality"],
["23","Work, free choice of employment, just and favourable conditions, protection against unemployment; equal pay for equal work; just remuneration; trade unions"],
["24","Rest and leisure, reasonable limitation of working hours, periodic holidays with pay"],
["25","A standard of living adequate for health and well-being — food, clothing, housing, medical care, social services; security in unemployment, sickness, disability, widowhood, old age; motherhood and childhood entitled to special care; equal protection for all children"],
["26","Education — free and compulsory at the elementary stage; technical and professional education generally available; higher education on merit; directed to the full development of personality and respect for human rights; parents' prior right to choose"],
["27","Participation in cultural life, the arts and scientific advancement; protection of moral and material interests in one's works"],
["28","A social and international order in which the rights can be fully realised"],
["29","Duties to the community; limitations only as determined by law for the rights of others, morality, public order and general welfare; never contrary to the UN's purposes"],
["30","No State, group or person may engage in any activity aimed at the destruction of the rights (the abuse-of-rights clause)"]
],[1100,7900]));
c.push(H3("Legal status"));
c.push(P("The UDHR was adopted as a **General Assembly resolution (217A (III))** — a 'declaration', not a treaty; it created no legal obligations at the time and Eleanor Roosevelt said so ('not a treaty... not an international agreement... not and does not purport to be a statement of law or of legal obligation'). Its status has grown by four routes. **(1) Customary international law**: State practice and opinio juris — the constant invocation of the Declaration by the General Assembly, the Security Council, the ICJ (the Tehran Hostages case, 1980, cited 'the fundamental principles enunciated in the Universal Declaration'), regional courts and national courts, and its incorporation in constitutions — have made at least its core (the prohibitions of slavery, torture, genocide, racial discrimination, arbitrary detention, the fair-trial and non-retroactivity guarantees) customary law binding on all States; the Restatement (Third) of US Foreign Relations Law §702 and Filartiga (1980) are the standard citations; the more contested view (Humphrey, Sohn) is that the whole Declaration is now customary. **(2) Authoritative interpretation of the Charter**: the Declaration gives content to the 'human rights and fundamental freedoms' of Arts 55-56, so that a member's obligations under the Charter are measured by it (the Proclamation of Tehran, 1968, para 2: 'The Universal Declaration... states a common understanding of the peoples of the world concerning the inalienable and inviolable rights of all members of the human family and constitutes an obligation for the members of the international community'). **(3) Incorporation in treaties**: the Covenants (whose preambles recall it), the regional conventions (the European Convention's preamble), the specialised conventions, the Helsinki Final Act (1975) and the 1993 Vienna Declaration reaffirm it, and their parties are bound to its standards through those treaties. **(4) National law**: over ninety constitutions written since 1948 draw on it (India's Part III and IV, 1949-50 — the Constituent Assembly's debates cite it; the Supreme Court in Kesavananda Bharati, 1973, and in Chairman, Railway Board v. Chandrima Das, 2000, treated its principles as applicable, and in Vishaka, 1997, read international norms into Art 21), and courts use it as an aid to interpretation. Together with the two Covenants and their protocols, the Declaration forms the **International Bill of Human Rights** — the Commission's 1946 plan completed in 1966 and in force in 1976."));
c.push(H3("Tehran 1968 and Vienna 1993"));
c.push(P("The **International Conference on Human Rights, Tehran (April-May 1968)** — the International Year for Human Rights, the twentieth anniversary — adopted the **Proclamation of Tehran**: the Declaration is an obligation for the international community (para 2); the gross denials of human rights under apartheid are a crime against humanity (para 7); the massive denials arising from aggression and armed conflict (para 10); the link between human rights and development — 'the achievement of lasting progress in the implementation of human rights is dependent upon sound and effective national and international policies of economic and social development' (para 13, the seed of the right to development); the rights of women (para 15) and the family's right to decide the number and spacing of children (para 16, the first international statement of reproductive rights); human rights in armed conflict (Resolution XXIII, which led the General Assembly to Resolution 2444 and the 1977 Protocols); the concern with science and technology, illiteracy, and youth. The **World Conference on Human Rights, Vienna (June 1993)** — 171 States, 800 NGOs, after the Cold War and the Asian-values challenge (the Bangkok Declaration of the Asian preparatory meeting had stressed sovereignty and particularity) — adopted the **Vienna Declaration and Programme of Action**: the universal nature of human rights is 'beyond question' (I.1); all human rights are universal, indivisible, interdependent and interrelated, and 'while the significance of national and regional particularities and various historical, cultural and religious backgrounds must be borne in mind, it is the duty of States, regardless of their political, economic and cultural systems, to promote and protect all human rights' (I.5); democracy, development and respect for human rights are interdependent (I.8); the right to development is a universal and inalienable right (I.10); the human rights of women and the girl-child are 'an inalienable, integral and indivisible part of universal human rights' (I.18) — violence against women, the appointment of a Special Rapporteur; the rights of the child, minorities, indigenous people, migrant workers, the disabled; the recommendation of a **High Commissioner for Human Rights** (II.18 — created by General Assembly Resolution 48/141, December 1993); national institutions under the Paris Principles (I.36); the strengthening of the treaty bodies; the universal ratification of the core treaties; and human rights education. Vienna is the constitutional moment of the post-Cold War system and the answer to relativism."));
c.push(H3("Criticisms and the relativist debate"));
c.push(P("(1) **Western origin** — drafted by a committee dominated by the Atlantic powers, in the language of liberal individualism (the American Anthropological Association's 1947 statement warned of ethnocentrism); the answer: the drafters included China, Lebanon, Chile, India, the Philippines and the USSR; the economic and social articles came from Latin America and the socialist States; the decolonised States endorsed it at Tehran and Vienna. (2) **Individualism vs community** — the 'Asian values' and African critiques that the Declaration ignores duties and the community (answered partly by Art 29 and by the African Charter's Chapter II duties). (3) **Religious objections** — to Art 18 (changing religion) and Art 16 (equal marriage rights) — the Cairo Declaration on Human Rights in Islam (1990) subordinated rights to Sharia; the 2004 Arab Charter is closer to the UDHR. (4) **Non-binding** — the enforcement gap; answered by the Covenants and the customary-law argument. (5) **Property and the socialist critique** — Art 17's protection of property and the absence of a right to self-determination (added in the Covenants' common Art 1). (6) **Feminist critique** — the public/private divide (answered by CEDAW and Vienna). (7) **Silence on minorities and indigenous peoples** (answered by Art 27 ICCPR, the 1992 and 2007 Declarations). The Declaration's defenders (Glendon's 'A World Made New', Morsink's history) show that it was a genuinely plural document, and the relativist debate has settled on Vienna's formula and on Sen's argument that the value of freedom is found in every tradition."));
c.push(CASE("United States Diplomatic and Consular Staff in Tehran (United States v. Iran), ICJ 1980","Wrongfully depriving human beings of their freedom and subjecting them to physical constraint in conditions of hardship is 'manifestly incompatible with the principles of the Charter of the United Nations, as well as with the fundamental principles enunciated in the Universal Declaration of Human Rights' — the Court's first reliance on the Declaration."));
c.push(CASE("Chairman, Railway Board v. Chandrima Das (2000) 2 SCC 465","A Bangladeshi woman gang-raped by railway employees at Howrah station was entitled to compensation under Art 21, which applies to non-citizens; the Court applied the Universal Declaration's principles (Arts 1, 2, 3, 5, 7, 9) as part of the law through Art 51(c) — 'the international covenants and declarations as adopted by the United Nations have to be respected by all signatory States and the meaning given to the above words in those declarations and covenants has to be such as would help in effective implementation of those rights'."));
c.push(CASE("Kesavananda Bharati v. State of Kerala (1973) 4 SCC 225 (Sikri CJ)","The Universal Declaration may not be a legally binding instrument but it shows how India understood the nature of human rights when the Constitution was adopted; the fundamental rights of Part III reflect it."));
c.push(CASE("Vishaka v. State of Rajasthan (1997) 6 SCC 241","In the absence of domestic law, international conventions and norms (CEDAW) not inconsistent with the fundamental rights are read into Arts 14, 15, 19 and 21 to enlarge their meaning — the rule for using the Declaration and the treaties in Indian courts."));
c.push(DIG("The UDHR's official translations exceed 550 languages (the OHCHR's record); the 75th anniversary (2023) produced the 'Human Rights 75' pledges; and the Declaration's Art 19 — 'through any media and regardless of frontiers' — is the text the Human Rights Council applies to the internet, while Art 12's privacy is the basis of the 2013 'right to privacy in the digital age' resolutions after the Snowden revelations."));
c.push(TIP("Part A: 'Universal Declaration of Human Rights', 'Legal status of the UDHR', 'International Bill of Human Rights', 'Proclamation of Tehran', 'Vienna Declaration 1993', 'Hansa Mehta', 'Art 25 UDHR', 'Art 29 UDHR'. Part B: 'Discuss the drafting, contents and legal significance of the Universal Declaration of Human Rights' — the story, Cassin's portico, the table's groups, the four routes to legal status, Tehran, Vienna, the Indian cases; or 'Is the Universal Declaration binding? Examine the customary-law argument'; or 'Compare the Proclamation of Tehran and the Vienna Declaration'. Part C: a State that never ratified the Covenants tortures a detainee — the prohibition of torture is customary law and jus cogens (Filartiga, Belgium v. Senegal), the UDHR's Art 5 binds; an Indian court is asked to apply Art 25 UDHR to a starvation death — Chandrima Das, Art 51(c), PUCL's food case under Art 21; a State argues the Declaration is Western and inapplicable to its culture — Vienna I.1 and I.5; the drafters' plurality; Sen."));
c.push(PB());

// ---------- EP 04 ----------
c.push(H1("Episode 04 — The Two Covenants: Toonen v Australia, the ICCPR and the ICESCR"));
c.push(H2("Covers: Why two Covenants — the Cold War split · The International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights — the rights, the limitation clauses, derogation (Art 4), the Human Rights Committee, the reporting and communication procedures, the two Optional Protocols · The International Covenant on Economic, Social and Cultural Rights — the rights, progressive realisation (Art 2(1)), minimum core, the CESCR, the 2008 Optional Protocol · India's ratification and reservations · The Covenants in Indian courts"));
c.push(STORY("Hobart, Tasmania, 1991. Nicholas Toonen was a gay activist in the one Australian State whose Criminal Code still made sex between consenting adult men a crime, punishable by twenty-one years. The Tasmanian Parliament would not repeal Sections 122 and 123, and the federal Government said it could not force it. Australia had ratified the First Optional Protocol to the International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights in 1991, and Toonen filed a communication with the Human Rights Committee in Geneva: the law, even unenforced, violated his privacy under Article 17 and discriminated against him contrary to Articles 2 and 26. Tasmania answered that the law protected public health and morals — the margin every State claims. The Committee's views of 31 March 1994 were the first international decision that sexual orientation is protected by human rights law. The criminalisation of private homosexual conduct was an 'arbitrary interference' with privacy; morality is not a matter exclusively of domestic concern when it is used to deny the rights of a minority; the reference to 'sex' in Articles 2 and 26 includes sexual orientation. The Committee recommended repeal. The Australian Parliament passed the Human Rights (Sexual Conduct) Act 1994 to override the Tasmanian provisions, and Tasmania repealed them in 1997. Toonen shows what the Covenant is — a treaty with a body that reads it, hears individuals, and expects States to comply — and what it is not — a court whose judgments bind; its 'views' worked in Australia because the federal Government chose to act, and Toonen's reasoning became a source for courts across the world, including the Delhi High Court in Naz Foundation (2009) and the Supreme Court in Navtej Singh Johar (2018), which cited it in reading down Section 377."));
c.push(H3("Why two Covenants"));
c.push(P("The Commission's plan of 1946 was a single convention; by 1951 the General Assembly, at Western insistence, had decided on two — the Western States arguing that civil and political rights are immediately enforceable, justiciable, 'negative' rights suited to a court-like machinery, while economic and social rights are programmatic, resource-dependent and best supervised through reports; the socialist and many developing States wanting one covenant on the ground of indivisibility (the 1950 Assembly resolution 421(V) had said the two sets of rights are 'interconnected and interdependent'). The compromise: two Covenants, adopted together on 16 December 1966 (Resolution 2200A (XXI)), with common Preambles and a common Art 1 (self-determination), Art 3 (equal rights of men and women) and Art 5 (no destruction of rights), to be opened for signature together. The ICESCR entered into force on 3 January 1976 and the ICCPR on 23 March 1976; today the ICCPR has 173 parties and the ICESCR 171; the United States has not ratified the ICESCR, China has not ratified the ICCPR; India ratified both on 10 April 1979."));
c.push(H3("The ICCPR — the rights"));
c.push(P("**Part I — Art 1**: all peoples have the right of self-determination; to freely dispose of their natural wealth and resources; the parties' duty to promote it. **Part II — the general provisions**: **Art 2** — each party undertakes to **respect and to ensure** to all individuals within its territory and subject to its jurisdiction the rights recognised, without distinction of any kind; to adopt laws and measures to give effect to them; and to ensure an **effective remedy** (the obligations of result and conduct; General Comment 31 on the nature of the obligation — including positive obligations and horizontal effect); **Art 3** — equal rights of men and women; **Art 4 — derogation**: in time of public emergency which threatens the life of the nation and the existence of which is officially proclaimed, the parties may take measures derogating from their obligations 'to the extent strictly required by the exigencies of the situation', provided the measures are not inconsistent with their other international obligations and do not discriminate solely on race, colour, sex, language, religion or social origin; **no derogation** from Arts 6 (life), 7 (torture), 8(1)-(2) (slavery), 11 (imprisonment for contract), 15 (retroactivity), 16 (legal personality) and 18 (thought, conscience, religion); notification to the Secretary-General (General Comment 29, 2001: the fair-trial and habeas corpus guarantees are also non-derogable in substance); **Art 5** — no destruction or excessive limitation of rights. **Part III — the substantive rights**: **Art 6** — the right to life, inherent, protected by law, no arbitrary deprivation; the death penalty only for the most serious crimes, by final judgment, not for persons under eighteen or pregnant women, with the right to seek pardon; genocide prohibited (General Comment 36, 2018); **Art 7** — no torture or cruel, inhuman or degrading treatment or punishment; no medical or scientific experimentation without consent; **Art 8** — no slavery, servitude, forced or compulsory labour; **Art 9** — liberty and security; no arbitrary arrest or detention; reasons and charges promptly; prompt production before a judge; trial within a reasonable time or release; habeas corpus; **compensation** for unlawful arrest (Art 9(5) — Nilabati Behera, 1993, applied it in India); **Art 10** — humane treatment of detainees; separation of accused from convicted, juveniles from adults; the aim of reformation; **Art 11** — no imprisonment for inability to fulfil a contractual obligation (Jolly George Varghese v. Bank of Cochin, 1980 — Sec 51 CPC read consistently); **Art 12** — liberty of movement and residence; the right to leave any country and to enter one's own; **Art 13** — expulsion of aliens only by law with a hearing; **Art 14** — fair trial: equality before courts; a fair and public hearing by a competent, independent and impartial tribunal; presumption of innocence; the minimum guarantees (information of the charge, time and facilities, trial without undue delay, presence and counsel, legal aid, witnesses, interpreter, no self-incrimination); juveniles; appeal; compensation for miscarriage of justice; no double jeopardy; **Art 15** — no retroactive criminal law; the lighter penalty; **Art 16** — recognition as a person; **Art 17** — no arbitrary or unlawful interference with privacy, family, home or correspondence, or attacks on honour (Toonen; PUCL v. Union of India, 1997 — telephone tapping); **Art 18** — thought, conscience and religion, including to adopt a religion; manifestation subject to limitations prescribed by law and necessary to protect public safety, order, health, morals or the rights of others; no coercion; parents' liberty; **Art 19** — opinion (absolute) and expression, including to seek, receive and impart information; restrictions only as provided by law and necessary for the rights or reputations of others, national security, public order, public health or morals (General Comment 34, 2011); **Art 20** — propaganda for war and advocacy of national, racial or religious hatred constituting incitement to be prohibited by law (India's reservation: only to the extent of Art 19 of the Constitution); **Art 21** — peaceful assembly; **Art 22** — association and trade unions; **Art 23** — the family; the right to marry with free and full consent; equality of spouses; **Art 24** — the child's right to protection, registration, name and nationality; **Art 25** — to take part in public affairs, to vote and be elected at genuine periodic elections by universal and equal suffrage and secret ballot, and equal access to public service; **Art 26** — equality before the law and equal protection; the law shall prohibit discrimination on any ground (a free-standing equality guarantee — Broeks v. Netherlands, 1987, applied it to social security); **Art 27** — persons belonging to ethnic, religious or linguistic minorities shall not be denied the right, in community with the other members, to enjoy their culture, profess their religion, or use their language (Lovelace v. Canada, 1981; Kitok; Lubicon Lake Band). **Part IV — the Human Rights Committee** (Arts 28-45); **Part V** — savings (Arts 46-47); **Part VI** — final clauses (Arts 48-53)."));
c.push(H3("The Human Rights Committee and its procedures"));
c.push(P("**Art 28-39**: eighteen members of high moral character and recognised competence in human rights, nationals of the parties, elected by the parties for four years in their personal capacity, with consideration to equitable geographical distribution and legal experience; meets three times a year (Geneva and New York). **Functions**: (1) **State reports** (Art 40) — within a year of entry into force and thereafter as requested (in practice every four to five years; India's last periodic report was considered in 1997, its fourth report submitted in 2019 and considered in July 2024 with concluding observations on the UAPA, sedition, the Manipur violence, the CAA, the death penalty and NHRC independence); the Committee examines the report in dialogue with the State's delegation and issues **concluding observations**; (2) **General Comments** (Art 40(4)) — authoritative interpretations of the articles (37 to date — GC 6 and 36 on life, 20 on torture, 29 on emergencies, 31 on the general obligation, 34 on expression, 35 on liberty, 37 on assembly); (3) **inter-State complaints** (Arts 41-43) — optional declaration; never used under the ICCPR; (4) **individual communications** under the **First Optional Protocol (1966; 117 parties; India not a party)** — a person subject to the jurisdiction of a party who claims to be a victim of a violation may submit a written communication after exhausting domestic remedies (unless unreasonably prolonged); inadmissible if anonymous, an abuse, incompatible with the Covenant, or being examined under another international procedure; the State responds within six months; the Committee adopts **'views'** — reasoned findings with recommended remedies — and follows up through a Special Rapporteur; the views are not legally binding as a judgment but are 'authoritative determinations' by the body established to interpret the treaty (GC 33, 2008) and a State that ignores them acts in bad faith; about 1,500 views adopted — Toonen, Lovelace, Broeks, the death-row cases against Jamaica and Trinidad (Pratt and Morgan), Ng v. Canada (gas asphyxiation), Judge v. Canada (extradition to face the death penalty), the Belarus and Uzbekistan cases, the 2019 Teitiota v. New Zealand climate-refugee view; (5) the **Second Optional Protocol (1989; 91 parties) — abolition of the death penalty**: no one within the jurisdiction of a party shall be executed; each party to abolish the death penalty; no reservation except for the most serious military crimes in wartime; India is not a party (the Supreme Court in Bachan Singh, 1980, upheld the death penalty for the 'rarest of rare', and the Covenant's Art 6 permits it)."));
c.push(H3("The ICESCR — the rights and the obligation"));
c.push(P("**Part I — Art 1** (self-determination, common). **Part II — Art 2(1)**: each party 'undertakes to take steps, individually and through international assistance and co-operation, especially economic and technical, **to the maximum of its available resources**, with a view to achieving **progressively the full realisation** of the rights recognised in the present Covenant by all appropriate means, including particularly the adoption of legislative measures' — the obligation of progressive realisation, explained by the CESCR's **General Comment 3 (1990)**: some obligations are immediate — non-discrimination (Art 2(2)), the duty to 'take steps' (deliberate, concrete, targeted), and a **minimum core obligation** to ensure at least minimum essential levels of each right (essential foodstuffs, primary health care, basic shelter, the most basic forms of education), failing which a State is prima facie in violation; retrogressive measures need full justification; resource constraints are no excuse for not protecting the vulnerable; the Limburg Principles (1986) and the Maastricht Guidelines (1997) elaborate the respect-protect-fulfil framework; **Art 2(2)** — non-discrimination; **Art 2(3)** — developing countries may determine the extent of economic rights for non-nationals; **Art 3** — equal rights of men and women; **Art 4** — limitations only by law, compatible with the nature of the rights, solely to promote the general welfare; **Art 5** — no destruction. **Part III — the rights**: **Art 6** — work, freely chosen, with training and policies for full employment; **Art 7** — just and favourable conditions — fair wages and equal remuneration for work of equal value, a decent living, safe and healthy conditions, equal promotion, rest, leisure, reasonable hours and holidays with pay; **Art 8** — trade unions, federations, the right to strike (India's reservation: subject to Art 19 of the Constitution); **Art 9** — social security including social insurance; **Art 10** — the family, marriage by free consent, maternity protection with paid leave, protection of children from exploitation and child labour; **Art 11** — an adequate standard of living including adequate **food, clothing and housing**, and the continuous improvement of living conditions; the fundamental right to be **free from hunger** (Art 11(2) — General Comment 12 on food, 4 and 7 on housing and forced evictions, 15 on water); **Art 12** — the highest attainable standard of physical and mental health (General Comment 14 — availability, accessibility, acceptability, quality); **Art 13** — education: free and compulsory primary education, secondary and higher education progressively free, fundamental education, the parents' liberty of choice, the liberty to establish institutions; **Art 14** — a plan for compulsory free primary education within two years where not yet secured; **Art 15** — cultural life, the benefits of scientific progress, authors' interests, the freedom of scientific research. **Part IV — Arts 16-25** — reporting to ECOSOC (the **Committee on Economic, Social and Cultural Rights**, created by ECOSOC in 1985, eighteen experts, examines reports every five years and issues General Comments — India's last review was in 2008; the next is pending). **The Optional Protocol (2008; in force 2013; 29 parties; India not a party)** — individual and group communications, inter-State complaints, and an inquiry procedure for grave or systematic violations — ended the argument that economic rights are non-justiciable internationally."));
c.push(H3("India and the Covenants"));
c.push(P("India signed and ratified both Covenants on **10 April 1979** with **declarations**: (1) on **Art 1** (self-determination) of both — it applies only to peoples under foreign domination and not to sovereign independent States or to a section of a people or nation, 'which is the essence of national integrity'; (2) on **Art 9** ICCPR — the right to compensation for unlawful arrest applies only under the Indian legal system (Nilabati Behera nonetheless applied Art 9(5)); (3) on **Art 13** — India reserves the right to apply its law relating to foreigners (the Foreigners Act 1946's expulsion power); (4) on **Arts 12, 19(3), 21 and 22** ICCPR and **Art 8** ICESCR — these are to be applied in conformity with Arts 19 (reasonable restrictions) and 22 of the Constitution, 'with respect to the rights and freedoms guaranteed'. India has **not** ratified the First or Second Optional Protocols to the ICCPR or the Optional Protocol to the ICESCR, and has not made the Art 41 declaration; it reports to the Committees (irregularly) and accepts the Universal Periodic Review. **In the courts**: the Covenants are not self-executing (India is dualist — Art 253 and Entry 14 of List I require legislation; the Protection of Human Rights Act 1993's definition of 'human rights' includes the rights 'embodied in the International Covenants' and 'enforceable by courts in India'), but the Supreme Court uses them to interpret the Constitution and to fill gaps: **Jolly George Varghese v. Bank of Cochin (1980)** (Art 11 ICCPR and Sec 51 CPC — no imprisonment of a debtor without means); **Nilabati Behera v. State of Orissa (1993)** (Art 9(5) — compensation for custodial death as a public-law remedy); **PUCL v. Union of India (1997)** (Art 17 — telephone tapping; the Court's guidelines); **Vishaka (1997)**; **Apparel Export Promotion Council v. A.K. Chopra (1999)** (CEDAW and the ILO on sexual harassment); **Chairman, Railway Board v. Chandrima Das (2000)**; **People's Union for Civil Liberties v. Union of India (2001-)** (the right to food case under Art 21 with Art 11 ICESCR in view); **Puttaswamy (2017)** (Art 17 ICCPR and Art 12 UDHR on privacy); **Navtej Singh Johar (2018)** (Toonen); **Kaushal Kishor v. State of U.P. (2023)** (horizontal application, citing General Comment 31). The rule of interpretation (Gramophone Co of India v. Birendra Bahadur Pandey, 1984; Vishaka; Puttaswamy): international law is part of domestic law unless inconsistent with an Act of Parliament, and the courts must interpret domestic law in harmony with India's treaty obligations."));
c.push(CASE("Toonen v. Australia, Human Rights Committee, Communication 488/1992 (1994)","Criminalisation of consensual adult homosexual conduct violates Art 17 (privacy) and Art 2(1); 'sex' in Arts 2 and 26 includes sexual orientation; moral objections cannot justify the violation."));
c.push(CASE("Lovelace v. Canada, HRC 24/1977 (1981)","A Maliseet woman who lost her Indian status on marrying a non-Indian was denied her Art 27 right to enjoy her culture in community with her group; Canada amended the Indian Act."));
c.push(CASE("Broeks v. Netherlands, HRC 172/1984 (1987)","Art 26 is an autonomous equality guarantee applying to all legislation, including social-security law outside the Covenant's civil and political scope; a rule requiring married women to prove breadwinner status was discriminatory."));
c.push(CASE("Jolly George Varghese v. Bank of Cochin (1980) 2 SCC 360","Art 11 ICCPR informs Sec 51 CPC: a judgment-debtor may not be imprisoned for mere inability to pay; Art 21's fair procedure requires proof of means or bad faith."));
c.push(CASE("Nilabati Behera v. State of Orissa (1993) 2 SCC 746","Compensation for custodial death as a public-law remedy under Arts 32 and 226; Art 9(5) ICCPR supports it and India's reservation does not bar the Court from applying the principle."));
c.push(CASE("People's Union for Civil Liberties v. Union of India (1997) 1 SCC 301","Telephone tapping under Sec 5(2) of the Telegraph Act infringes privacy under Art 21 and Art 17 ICCPR unless done by the procedure the Court laid down; international covenants can be read into domestic law where not inconsistent."));
c.push(DIG("The treaty bodies' jurisprudence is on the OHCHR's Jurisprudence Database; the Committees hold online sessions and accept digital submissions; the Human Rights Committee's 2024 review of India is on the UN Web TV archive; and General Comment 34's application of Art 19 to the internet, the 2021 GC 37 on online assembly, and the Committee's views on internet shutdowns are the digital extension of the Covenant."));
c.push(TIP("Part A: 'Why two Covenants', 'Derogation under Art 4 ICCPR', 'Non-derogable rights', 'Human Rights Committee', 'First Optional Protocol', 'Second Optional Protocol', 'Progressive realisation', 'Minimum core obligation', 'India's reservations to the Covenants', 'Toonen case'. Part B: 'Discuss the rights guaranteed by the ICCPR and the machinery for their implementation'; or 'Explain the nature of the obligations under the ICESCR with reference to Art 2(1) and General Comment 3'; or 'Compare the two Covenants'; or 'How have the Covenants been used by Indian courts?' — the cases. Part C: a State declares an emergency and suspends the prohibition of torture — Art 4(2), non-derogable; a State imprisons a person for a civil debt — Art 11, Jolly George Varghese; a State party to the First Optional Protocol ignores the Committee's views — no legal sanction, but bad faith (GC 33), follow-up procedure; an Indian citizen wants to complain to the Human Rights Committee — India is not a party to the Protocol; the remedy is the NHRC and the courts; a State says it cannot afford primary education — Art 13-14 ICESCR, minimum core, Unni Krishnan and Art 21A."));
c.push(PB());

// ---------- EP 05 ----------
c.push(H1("Episode 05 — Death Row and the Strasbourg Court: Soering and the European Convention on Human Rights"));
c.push(H2("Covers: The Council of Europe and the Convention of 1950 · The rights and the limitation clauses · The machinery — the Commission and Court to 1998, the permanent Court after Protocol 11, Protocols 14-16 · The doctrines — margin of appreciation, proportionality, positive obligations, the living instrument · Landmark cases · The European Social Charter · The European Union — the Charter of Fundamental Rights and the Court of Justice of the European Union · The relation between the two European courts"));
c.push(STORY("Bedford, England, 1986. Jens Soering, an eighteen-year-old German student at the University of Virginia, and his girlfriend Elizabeth Haysom fled to England after the murder of her parents in Bedford County, Virginia. Arrested for cheque fraud in London, Soering confessed to the murders; Virginia sought his extradition to face capital murder charges, and the United States gave the customary assurance that the judge would be told of the United Kingdom's wish that the death penalty not be imposed — an assurance the Home Secretary accepted. Soering's lawyers went to Strasbourg. The Convention did not then prohibit the death penalty (Protocol 6 had not been ratified by the UK), and extradition was a matter for the treaty between the two States; but Article 3 prohibited inhuman or degrading treatment, and Soering argued that six to eight years on Virginia's death row, in the shadow of execution, was itself such treatment — the 'death row phenomenon'. On 7 July 1989 the European Court of Human Rights unanimously agreed. A Contracting State that extradites a person to face a real risk of treatment contrary to Article 3 in the receiving State is itself responsible; the Convention's protection does not stop at the border; and the conditions on Virginia's death row, in the light of Soering's age and mental state, would cross the threshold. Britain could not extradite him unless the capital charge was dropped; it was; Soering was extradited, convicted of first-degree murder, served thirty-three years and was paroled in 2019, still protesting his innocence. Soering is the case every student learns for three propositions: the extra-territorial reach of the Convention, the absolute character of Article 3, and the Court's method — a living instrument, read in the light of present-day conditions."));
c.push(H3("The Council of Europe and the Convention"));
c.push(P("The **Council of Europe** (Treaty of London, 5 May 1949; ten founders; now 46 members after Russia's expulsion in March 2022) is the intergovernmental organisation for democracy, human rights and the rule of law, distinct from the European Union; its organs are the Committee of Ministers, the Parliamentary Assembly, the Secretary General and the Commissioner for Human Rights (1999). Its first great act was the **Convention for the Protection of Human Rights and Fundamental Freedoms** (Rome, 4 November 1950; in force 3 September 1953) — the first binding treaty to give effect to the UDHR's civil and political rights, drafted quickly under the shadow of Stalinism, with Britain's David Maxwell-Fyfe and France's Pierre-Henri Teitgen as its authors, and with a machinery of collective enforcement: a Commission, a Court and the Committee of Ministers. **The rights** (Section I): Art 2 life (with the original death-penalty exception); Art 3 no torture or inhuman or degrading treatment (absolute); Art 4 no slavery or forced labour; Art 5 liberty and security (with an exhaustive list of permitted detentions, habeas corpus and compensation); Art 6 fair trial (civil and criminal — the most litigated article); Art 7 no punishment without law; Art 8 private and family life, home and correspondence; Art 9 thought, conscience and religion; Art 10 expression; Art 11 assembly and association; Art 12 marriage; Art 13 an effective remedy; Art 14 non-discrimination in the enjoyment of the Convention rights (not free-standing — Protocol 12 adds a general prohibition); Art 15 derogation in war or public emergency (not from Arts 2 (except lawful acts of war), 3, 4(1), 7); Art 16 restrictions on aliens' political activity; Art 17 no abuse of rights; Art 18 limitations only for the prescribed purposes. **The Protocols** add rights: Protocol 1 (1952) — property, education, free elections; Protocol 4 (1963) — no imprisonment for debt, movement, no expulsion of nationals, no collective expulsion of aliens; Protocol 6 (1983) — abolition of the death penalty in peacetime; Protocol 7 (1984) — procedural safeguards for expulsion of aliens, appeal in criminal matters, compensation for wrongful conviction, ne bis in idem, equality of spouses; Protocol 12 (2000) — general prohibition of discrimination; Protocol 13 (2002) — abolition of the death penalty in all circumstances. **The limitation clauses** of Arts 8-11 share a structure: an interference must be (1) 'in accordance with the law' / 'prescribed by law' (accessible, foreseeable), (2) in pursuit of a legitimate aim in the exhaustive list (national security, public safety, the economic well-being of the country, the prevention of disorder or crime, the protection of health or morals, the protection of the rights and freedoms of others), and (3) 'necessary in a democratic society' — a pressing social need, proportionality, relevant and sufficient reasons — the test that Indian courts adopted in Modern Dental College and Puttaswamy."));
c.push(H3("The machinery — from the Commission to the permanent Court"));
c.push(P("**1953-1998**: the **European Commission of Human Rights** (1954) received applications, decided admissibility, found facts, sought friendly settlement and reported an opinion; the **European Court of Human Rights** (1959) heard cases referred by the Commission or a State (individuals could not go to the Court until Protocol 9 in 1994); the **Committee of Ministers** decided cases the Court did not receive and supervised execution. The right of individual petition (old Art 25) and the Court's jurisdiction (old Art 46) were optional — Britain accepted both in 1966, France in 1981. **Protocol 11 (in force 1 November 1998)** abolished the Commission and the Committee's judicial role and created a single, full-time, permanent **European Court of Human Rights** in Strasbourg, with compulsory jurisdiction over individual applications (Art 34) and inter-State cases (Art 33) for all parties; judges (one per State, elected by the Parliamentary Assembly for a nine-year non-renewable term since Protocol 14) sit in single-judge formations (inadmissibility), committees of three (repetitive cases), Chambers of seven and the Grand Chamber of seventeen (Arts 26-31); **admissibility** (Art 35): exhaustion of domestic remedies, four months from the final decision (six before Protocol 15, 2021), not anonymous, not substantially the same as a matter already examined, not incompatible, manifestly ill-founded or an abuse, and (Protocol 14) the applicant has suffered a 'significant disadvantage'; **judgments** (Art 41 — just satisfaction; Art 44 — final; Art 46 — binding on the parties, supervised by the Committee of Ministers, which since Protocol 14 may bring infringement proceedings); **pilot judgments** (Broniowski v. Poland, 2004) for systemic problems; **interim measures** (Rule 39 — Mamatkulov v. Turkey, 2005, made them binding); **Protocol 16 (2018)** — advisory opinions at the request of the highest national courts. The docket: about 60,000 pending applications (from a peak of 160,000 in 2011), most from Russia (until 2022), Turkey, Ukraine and Romania; over 25,000 judgments since 1959. The Court's remedies are declaratory plus damages; the State chooses the means of compliance (individual measures — reopening, release; general measures — legislation), and the Committee of Ministers' supervision is the enforcement — with the United Kingdom's prisoner-voting refusal (Hirst, 2005-2018) and Russia's 2015 constitutional-court doctrine of non-execution as the limits."));
c.push(H3("The doctrines"));
c.push(P("(1) **The margin of appreciation** — the latitude the Court allows national authorities in assessing the necessity of an interference, wider where there is no European consensus and in matters of morals, religion and national security (Handyside v. UK, 1976 — the Little Red Schoolbook; the authorities are 'in principle in a better position than the international judge to give an opinion on the exact content of these requirements'), narrower for the core of a right or where consensus exists; Protocol 15 (2021) inserted it into the Preamble with the principle of subsidiarity. (2) **Proportionality** — the fair balance between the general interest and the individual's rights, with the least restrictive means (Sporrong and Lönnroth, 1982). (3) **Positive obligations** — the State must not only refrain but act: to protect life from known risks (Osman v. UK, 1998), to investigate deaths and torture (McCann v. UK, 1995 — the Gibraltar shootings; Assenov v. Bulgaria, 1998), to protect private life against private parties (X and Y v. Netherlands, 1985; horizontal effect), to protect against domestic violence (Opuz v. Turkey, 2009), to prevent environmental harm (López Ostra v. Spain, 1994). (4) **The living instrument** — the Convention is interpreted 'in the light of present-day conditions' (Tyrer v. UK, 1978 — judicial birching in the Isle of Man degrading; Marckx v. Belgium, 1979 — illegitimate children; Goodwin v. UK, 2002 — transsexuals' right to marry, overruling earlier cases). (5) **Autonomous concepts** — 'civil rights and obligations', 'criminal charge' (Engel v. Netherlands, 1976) have Convention meanings independent of national labels. (6) **Effectiveness** — rights that are 'practical and effective, not theoretical and illusory' (Airey v. Ireland, 1979 — legal aid for a separation petition). (7) **Extra-territoriality** — jurisdiction under Art 1 extends to territory under effective control (Loizidou v. Turkey, 1995; Al-Skeini v. UK, 2011 — Iraq) and to the consequences of expulsion (Soering; Chahal v. UK, 1996 — deportation of a Sikh separatist to India barred under Art 3 regardless of national-security grounds; Saadi v. Italy, 2008). (8) **Absolute rights** — Art 3 admits no balancing (Ireland v. UK, 1978 — the five techniques were inhuman and degrading, not torture; revisited 2018; Gäfgen v. Germany, 2010 — threats of torture to find a kidnapped child violated Art 3). (9) **Subsidiarity and the fourth-instance rule** — the Court does not re-try facts or national law. (10) **Erga omnes effect of the case law** — the res interpretata that binds all States in practice."));
c.push(H3("Landmark cases — a selection"));
c.push(TBL([
["Case","Holding"],
["Lawless v. Ireland (1961)","The first judgment; Art 15 derogation for the IRA emergency upheld; 'public emergency threatening the life of the nation' defined"],
["Golder v. UK (1975)","Art 6 includes a right of access to a court; a prisoner refused permission to consult a solicitor"],
["Handyside v. UK (1976)","Art 10 protects information and ideas that 'offend, shock or disturb'; the margin of appreciation on morals"],
["Tyrer v. UK (1978)","Judicial corporal punishment is degrading; the living instrument"],
["Sunday Times v. UK (1979)","The thalidomide injunction violated Art 10; 'prescribed by law' requires accessibility and foreseeability; the press's watchdog role"],
["Dudgeon v. UK (1981)","Northern Ireland's criminalisation of homosexual acts violated Art 8 — the precursor of Toonen and Navtej Johar"],
["Soering v. UK (1989)","Extradition to face the death-row phenomenon violates Art 3; extra-territorial responsibility"],
["McCann v. UK (1995)","The right to life includes a duty of effective investigation and of planning operations to minimise lethal force"],
["Chahal v. UK (1996)","Art 3 is absolute; no deportation to a real risk of ill-treatment even on national-security grounds"],
["Osman v. UK (1998)","Positive obligation to protect life where the authorities knew or ought to have known of a real and immediate risk"],
["Goodwin v. UK (2002)","Transsexual persons' rights to legal recognition and marriage under Arts 8 and 12"],
["Hirst v. UK (No 2) (2005)","A blanket ban on prisoners' voting violates Art 3 of Protocol 1; the UK's thirteen-year non-compliance"],
["Öcalan v. Turkey (2005); Al-Saadoon v. UK (2010)","The death penalty after an unfair trial is inhuman; Art 2 and 3 read with Protocol 13 now bar the death penalty in all circumstances"],
["Al-Skeini v. UK (2011)","The Convention applies to British forces' conduct in Iraq — jurisdiction through State agents' authority and control"],
["S.A.S. v. France (2014)","The face-veil ban upheld under 'living together' — a wide margin on religious symbols"],
["Big Brother Watch v. UK (2021)","Bulk interception regimes need end-to-end safeguards; the digital-surveillance standard"],
["Verein KlimaSeniorinnen v. Switzerland (2024)","Inadequate climate policy violated Art 8 — the first climate-change judgment of an international human-rights court"]
],[3000,6000]));
c.push(H3("The European Social Charter"));
c.push(P("The **European Social Charter (Turin, 1961; revised 1996)** is the Council of Europe's economic and social counterpart: the rights to work, just conditions, safe conditions, fair remuneration, to organise and bargain collectively, protection of children and young persons, of employed women, vocational guidance and training, health, social security, social and medical assistance, social welfare services, of the disabled, of the family, of mothers and children, to engage in gainful occupation in other parties' territory, of migrant workers, and (1996) to equal opportunities, information and consultation, dignity at work, housing, protection against poverty and social exclusion; States choose a core of articles to accept ('à la carte'); supervision is by the **European Committee of Social Rights** through national reports and, under the 1995 Additional Protocol, **collective complaints** by trade unions, employers' organisations and NGOs (not individuals) — the Committee's 'decisions on the merits' (the Roma housing cases against Greece, Italy and France; the Irish and Finnish cases on corporal punishment and social security; the 2012-14 Greek austerity decisions) are followed up by the Committee of Ministers. The Charter and the Convention together are the European 'two covenants'."));
c.push(H3("The European Union — the Charter and the Court of Justice"));
c.push(P("The syllabus's 'European Court of Justice' is the court of the **European Union** — a separate organisation (the Treaty of Rome 1957; the Maastricht Treaty 1992; the Lisbon Treaty 2009; 27 members) whose **Court of Justice of the European Union** (CJEU, Luxembourg — the Court of Justice and the General Court) interprets and applies EU law, chiefly through **preliminary references** from national courts (Art 267 TFEU), infringement actions against States (Art 258) and annulment actions (Art 263). The EU's treaties originally said nothing of human rights; the Court of Justice, faced with the German Constitutional Court's threat to review Community law against German fundamental rights (Internationale Handelsgesellschaft, 1970; Solange I, 1974), declared that fundamental rights form part of the **general principles of Community law** which the Court protects, drawing on the constitutional traditions common to the member States and on international instruments, in particular the ECHR (Stauder, 1969; Nold, 1974; Hauer, 1979); the Maastricht Treaty (Art F, now Art 6 TEU) codified it; the **Charter of Fundamental Rights of the European Union** (Nice, 2000) became legally binding with the Lisbon Treaty (Art 6(1) TEU — the same legal value as the Treaties) — 54 articles in seven titles (dignity, freedoms, equality, solidarity, citizens' rights, justice, general provisions), including social rights, data protection (Art 8), a right to good administration (Art 41) and the environment; it binds the EU's institutions and the member States 'only when they are implementing Union law' (Art 51); Art 52(3) aligns its rights with the ECHR's. The Court's human-rights jurisprudence: **Kadi v. Council and Commission (2008)** — the EU regulation implementing UN Security Council terrorist-asset freezes annulled for violating the right to be heard, effective judicial protection and property — EU law's fundamental-rights guarantees prevail even over Chapter VII obligations as implemented in the EU order; **Digital Rights Ireland (2014)** — the Data Retention Directive invalid under Arts 7 and 8 of the Charter; **Google Spain (2014)** — the right to be forgotten; **Schrems I and II (2015, 2020)** — the invalidation of the EU-US data-transfer frameworks for inadequate protection against US surveillance; **Tele2 Sverige (2016)**, **La Quadrature du Net (2020)** — limits on general data retention; **Coman (2018)** — same-sex spouses' free movement; **Commission v. Poland (2019-23)** — judicial independence under Art 19 TEU and the rule-of-law conditionality. **The relationship between the two courts**: the EU is not a party to the ECHR (Lisbon's Art 6(2) requires accession; the CJEU's Opinion 2/13 (2014) rejected the draft accession agreement as incompatible with the autonomy of EU law; negotiations resumed in 2020); the Strasbourg Court presumes that EU law provides 'equivalent protection' (Bosphorus v. Ireland, 2005) and reviews only manifest deficiencies; the CJEU treats the ECHR as a source of general principles and the Charter's floor; national courts must apply both; and the CJEU's Kadi and the ECtHR's Al-Dulimi (2016) together assert that even UN-mandated measures must respect fundamental rights in Europe."));
c.push(CASE("Soering v. United Kingdom (1989) 11 EHRR 439","Extradition to face the death-row phenomenon would violate Art 3; the Convention's protection extends to the foreseeable consequences of removal; Art 3 admits no balancing against extradition interests."));
c.push(CASE("Handyside v. United Kingdom (1976) 1 EHRR 737","Freedom of expression protects ideas that offend, shock or disturb; the margin of appreciation is widest on morals; the Court's supervision remains."));
c.push(CASE("Chahal v. United Kingdom (1996) 23 EHRR 413","No deportation to India of a Sikh militant facing a real risk of torture; Art 3 is absolute and national security cannot outweigh it; indefinite detention without effective review violated Art 5(4)."));
c.push(CASE("Ireland v. United Kingdom (1978) 2 EHRR 25","The five interrogation techniques were inhuman and degrading treatment; the definition of torture as an aggravated form; inter-State applications."));
c.push(CASE("Kadi and Al Barakaat v. Council and Commission, CJEU 2008","EU measures implementing UN sanctions must respect fundamental rights — the right to be heard, judicial review, property; the EU legal order's autonomy; annulment of the regulation as applied to Kadi."));
c.push(CASE("Bosphorus Airways v. Ireland (2005) 42 EHRR 1","The Strasbourg Court presumes that a State complying with EU law respects the Convention because EU law provides equivalent protection, unless the protection is manifestly deficient."));
c.push(DIG("The HUDOC database publishes every judgment and decision; the Court accepts applications by a mandatory form and hears cases online; the CJEU's Schrems and data-retention judgments govern the data flows of Indian IT companies serving Europe (the GDPR's adequacy regime); and the KlimaSeniorinnen and Big Brother Watch judgments are the current frontier of environmental and surveillance rights."));
c.push(TIP("Part A: 'European Convention on Human Rights', 'European Court of Human Rights after Protocol 11', 'Margin of appreciation', 'Soering case', 'Positive obligations', 'European Social Charter', 'Charter of Fundamental Rights of the EU', 'Kadi case', 'Difference between ECtHR and CJEU'. Part B: 'Describe the rights protected by the European Convention and the machinery for their enforcement, and explain why the European system is considered the most effective' — the rights, Protocol 11, admissibility, binding judgments, the Committee of Ministers, the doctrines; or 'Discuss the role of the Court of Justice of the European Union in the protection of human rights' — the general principles, the Charter, Kadi, Digital Rights Ireland, the ECHR relationship. Part C: a State extradites a person to face torture — Soering, Chahal, absolute Art 3; a State suspends fair-trial rights during a terrorist emergency — Art 15, Lawless, proportionality, non-derogable core; a State's highest court asks the Strasbourg Court for an opinion — Protocol 16; a national court applies an EU regulation violating privacy — the Charter's Art 7-8, a preliminary reference to the CJEU (Digital Rights Ireland); India seeks a fugitive's extradition from a Convention State — the assurances (no death penalty, no torture, prison conditions) that Chahal and Soering require, as in the Vijay Mallya and Nirav Modi cases in the UK courts."));
c.push(PB());

// ---------- EP 06 ----------
c.push(H1("Episode 06 — The Disappeared: Velásquez Rodríguez, the American Convention and the African Charter"));
c.push(H2("Covers: The Inter-American system — the OAS, the American Declaration (1948), the American Convention (1969), the Commission and the Court, the doctrines of due diligence and conventionality control · The African system — the OAU/AU, the African Charter on Human and Peoples' Rights (1981), peoples' rights and duties, the Commission, the African Court · The Arab Charter and the ASEAN Declaration · Why Asia has no regional system · Comparison of the three systems"));
c.push(STORY("Tegucigalpa, Honduras, 12 September 1981. Angel Manfredo Velásquez Rodríguez, a graduate student, was seized in a parking lot by seven heavily armed men in civilian clothes driving a white Ford without plates — the method, witnesses said, of Battalion 3-16, the army's intelligence unit. He was never seen again. Between 1981 and 1984 more than a hundred Hondurans disappeared the same way; the courts issued habeas corpus writs that the army ignored; no official was ever charged. His family's petition reached the Inter-American Commission on Human Rights in Washington, which found the State responsible and, when Honduras did nothing, referred the case to the Inter-American Court in San José, Costa Rica — the Court's first contentious case. Honduras denied everything, and during the proceedings two of the witnesses who had testified were murdered. On 29 July 1988 the Court held that forced disappearance is a multiple and continuing violation — of liberty (Art 7), of humane treatment (Art 5) and of life (Art 4) — and, more importantly for the law of every system, that a State is responsible not only for what its agents do but for what it fails to do: it must organise its apparatus so as to ensure the free and full exercise of rights; it must prevent, investigate and punish every violation, and compensate the victim; and where a private or unidentified actor commits the act, the State's failure to exercise **due diligence** to prevent or respond makes it responsible. The Court ordered Honduras to pay the family. The 'due diligence' standard travelled into the CEDAW Committee's General Recommendation 19 on violence against women, into the European Court's Osman and Opuz, into the UN's 1992 and 2006 instruments on disappearances, and into the Indian Supreme Court's custodial-violence jurisprudence. The case is the Americas' contribution to the world."));
c.push(H3("The Inter-American system"));
c.push(P("The **Organization of American States** (Bogotá Charter, 1948; 35 members) adopted at the same conference the **American Declaration of the Rights and Duties of Man** (May 1948 — seven months before the UDHR, the first international human-rights instrument, with a chapter of duties); the **Inter-American Commission on Human Rights** was created in 1959 (Santiago) as an OAS organ and, through the 1967 Protocol of Buenos Aires, a principal organ of the Charter, so that it may examine the human-rights situation in every OAS member (including the United States, Cuba and Canada, which have not ratified the Convention) against the Declaration; the **American Convention on Human Rights** ('Pact of San José', 22 November 1969; in force 18 July 1978; 23 parties — Venezuela denounced in 2012, Trinidad in 1998; the United States signed but never ratified) — civil and political rights modelled on the ICCPR and the ECHR, with distinctive features: the right to life 'in general, from the moment of conception' (Art 4(1)), no reintroduction of the death penalty once abolished and no extension (Art 4(2)-(3)), the right to a name, nationality and to reply (Arts 18, 20, 14), the rights of the child and the family, property (Art 21), the right to asylum (Art 22(7)) and the non-refoulement rule (22(8)), the prohibition of collective expulsion, progressive development of economic and social rights (Art 26), a suspension clause listing non-derogable rights including the 'judicial guarantees essential for the protection of such rights' (Art 27), and the **Protocol of San Salvador** (1988; in force 1999) on economic, social and cultural rights with the rights to education and to organise justiciable by petition; the **Protocol on the Abolition of the Death Penalty** (1990); and the specialised conventions — torture (1985), forced disappearance (1994), violence against women (Belém do Pará, 1994 — the first binding treaty on violence against women), the disabled (1999), racism (2013), older persons (2015). **The Commission** (seven members, Washington): receives **petitions** from any person, group or NGO (Art 44 — no victim requirement, unlike Strasbourg), against any party or against non-party members under the Declaration; admissibility (exhaustion, six months, no duplication); friendly settlement; a report with recommendations (Art 50); and, if the State does not comply, referral to the Court (Art 51 — since 2001 the Commission refers most cases) or publication; **precautionary measures** (the request to Colombia for the Awá community, to the US on Guantánamo); country reports and on-site visits (Argentina 1979 — the report that exposed the disappearances; Chile, Nicaragua, Haiti); thematic rapporteurs (freedom of expression, women, indigenous peoples, migrants); hearings. **The Court** (seven judges, San José; 1979): **contentious jurisdiction** (Art 62 — optional, accepted by 20 States; only the Commission and States may bring cases, the victim's representatives participating since 2001) with binding judgments (Art 68) awarding **reparations** — the Court's most creative feature: compensation, restitution, rehabilitation, satisfaction (public apologies, monuments, naming streets, publishing the judgment, scholarships), guarantees of non-repetition (legislative reform, training), the investigation and punishment of perpetrators — and **provisional measures** (Art 63(2)); **advisory jurisdiction** (Art 64 — the widest of any human-rights court: any OAS member or organ may ask about the Convention or any other human-rights treaty in the Americas — OC-5 on compulsory licensing of journalists, OC-16 on consular assistance as due process (relied on in Avena), OC-18 on migrants, OC-24 on gender identity and same-sex marriage, OC-23 on the environment); **compliance supervision** by the Court itself (not a political body), with compliance hearings. **Doctrines**: (1) **due diligence** (Velásquez Rodríguez); (2) the **continuing violation** of disappearance; (3) the invalidity of **amnesty laws** for grave violations (Barrios Altos v. Peru, 2001 — the self-amnesty laws 'lack legal effect'; Gomes Lund v. Brazil, 2010; Gelman v. Uruguay, 2011); (4) **conventionality control** — every national judge must apply the Convention as interpreted by the Court and disapply contrary national law (Almonacid Arellano v. Chile, 2006); (5) the **pro homine** principle (Art 29 — the most favourable interpretation); (6) **indigenous land rights** as property under Art 21 (Awas Tingni v. Nicaragua, 2001; Saramaka v. Suriname, 2007; Sarayaku v. Ecuador, 2012 — free, prior and informed consultation); (7) the **death penalty** limits (Hilaire v. Trinidad, 2002 — mandatory death penalty arbitrary; the 'death row' cases); (8) **freedom of expression** (Herrera Ulloa v. Costa Rica, 2004 — criminal defamation of public officials; the Declaration of Principles on Freedom of Expression, 2000); (9) **gender violence** (González 'Cotton Field' v. Mexico, 2009 — the Ciudad Juárez femicides; the State's failure of due diligence). The system's weakness: the United States' and Canada's non-participation in the Court, the Commission's chronic underfunding and backlog, the denunciations (Venezuela, Trinidad), and compliance rates below the European system's — but its jurisprudence on reparations, amnesties, disappearances and indigenous rights leads the world."));
c.push(H3("The African system"));
c.push(P("The **Organisation of African Unity** (1963; replaced by the **African Union**, Constitutive Act 2000, in force 2001; 55 members) adopted the **African Charter on Human and Peoples' Rights** at Nairobi on 27 June 1981 (the 'Banjul Charter', drafted at Banjul; in force 21 October 1986; ratified by all AU members except the newest, Morocco, which acceded in 2017 — 54 parties). Its distinctive features: (1) it contains **civil and political, economic, social and cultural, and peoples' rights in one document** — indivisibility in text (Preamble: civil and political rights cannot be dissociated from economic, social and cultural rights); (2) **peoples' rights** (Arts 19-24): equality of peoples, self-determination and freedom from domination (Art 20), free disposal of wealth and natural resources with a right to recovery of property in case of spoliation (Art 21), economic, social and cultural development (Art 22), national and international peace and security (Art 23), 'a general satisfactory environment favourable to their development' (Art 24) — the third generation in binding form; (3) **duties** (Chapter II, Arts 27-29): the individual's duties towards the family and society, the State, other legally recognised communities and the international community; to exercise rights with due regard to the rights of others, collective security, morality and common interest; to preserve the harmonious development of the family, to respect parents, to serve the national community, not to compromise the security of the State, to preserve and strengthen social and national solidarity and independence, to work, to pay taxes, to preserve African cultural values, and to contribute to African unity — the communitarian conception, criticised as a licence for the State (the Commission's Kenya and Nigeria cases read duties as not limiting rights); (4) **'claw-back' clauses** — several rights are guaranteed 'within the law' or 'subject to law and order' (Arts 6, 8, 9(2), 10, 12, 13, 14), which the Commission neutralised by reading 'law' as international law and applying Art 27(2)'s limitation only for legitimate interests (Media Rights Agenda v. Nigeria, 1998; Constitutional Rights Project v. Nigeria); (5) **no derogation clause** — the Commission holds that the Charter cannot be derogated from even in emergencies (Commission Nationale des Droits de l'Homme v. Chad, 1995); (6) the economic and social rights are immediately stated (education, health, work, the family, the aged and disabled — Arts 15-18) without a progressive-realisation qualifier; (7) the **right to development** (Art 22) as a binding right (Endorois v. Kenya, 2009 — the first finding of a violation of the right to development). **The African Commission on Human and Peoples' Rights** (Banjul, 1987; eleven members): promotion (studies, seminars, resolutions, guidelines — the Robben Island Guidelines on torture, the Principles on Freedom of Expression), protection through **communications** from States (Art 47-54 — the DRC v. Burundi, Rwanda and Uganda case, 2003) and from individuals and NGOs (Art 55 — 'other communications', developed into a full petition procedure with admissibility under Art 56, including exhaustion and a reasonable time), reports to the AU Assembly (whose non-publication of the Commission's findings was the early weakness), State reports every two years (Art 62), on-site missions, special rapporteurs and working groups (indigenous populations, extractive industries, the death penalty); the leading decisions: **SERAC and CESR v. Nigeria (2001)** — the Ogoni case: the oil operations in Ogoniland violated the rights to health, environment (Art 24), housing (implied from Arts 14, 16, 18) and food (implied), and the peoples' right to natural resources (Art 21), with the respect-protect-promote-fulfil framework; **Endorois v. Kenya (2009)** — eviction from ancestral land around Lake Bogoria violated property, culture, religion, natural resources and development; **Ken Saro-Wiwa (International PEN v. Nigeria, 1998)** — the trial and execution violated Arts 4, 5, 6, 7; **Purohit and Moore v. The Gambia (2003)** — mental-health law; **Zimbabwe Lawyers for Human Rights** — judicial independence; the Commission's 2011 referral of Libya to the Court. **The African Court on Human and Peoples' Rights** (Protocol of 1998; in force 2004; seated at Arusha; eleven judges; 34 parties): contentious jurisdiction over cases from the Commission, States and African intergovernmental organisations, and — only against the (now eight, after Tanzania, Benin, Côte d'Ivoire and Rwanda withdrew) States that have made the Art 34(6) declaration — directly from individuals and NGOs; advisory opinions; binding judgments with reparations supervised by the AU Executive Council; first judgment in 2009 (Yogogombaye v. Senegal — inadmissible for lack of the declaration); **Mtikila v. Tanzania (2013)** — the ban on independent candidates violated Arts 2, 10 and 13; **African Commission v. Kenya (Ogiek case, 2017)** — the first indigenous-rights judgment; **Konaté v. Burkina Faso (2014)** — imprisonment for defamation of public officials disproportionate; the 2008 Protocol to merge it with the AU Court of Justice and the 2014 **Malabo Protocol** adding an international-criminal section (with immunity for sitting heads of State — not in force). Add the **African Charter on the Rights and Welfare of the Child (1990)** with its Committee, the **Maputo Protocol on the Rights of Women (2003)**, the **Kampala Convention on internally displaced persons (2009)** — the world's first binding IDP treaty — and the **Peer Review Mechanism** of NEPAD (2003) as the continent's contributions. The system's weaknesses: the AU's political shielding of leaders (the ICC withdrawal threats after the Al Bashir warrant; the Malabo immunity), the withdrawals of the Art 34(6) declarations, the low compliance with the Commission's decisions and the Court's judgments (Tanzania's non-compliance with over 30 judgments), and the underfunding; its strengths, the Charter's holism and the Commission's creative jurisprudence."));
c.push(H3("The Arab, ASEAN and Asian situation"));
c.push(P("The **League of Arab States'** **Arab Charter on Human Rights** (1994, never in force; revised 2004, in force 2008; 16 parties) reflects the UDHR and the Covenants but with provisions the High Commissioner criticised (the death penalty for children in some contexts; the equation of Zionism with racism in the preamble; the reference to Sharia); the **Arab Human Rights Committee** reviews reports; the 2014 Statute of an Arab Court of Human Rights has not entered into force. The **Organisation of Islamic Cooperation's** Cairo Declaration (1990, revised 2020) and its Independent Permanent Human Rights Commission (2011) are declaratory. The **Association of Southeast Asian Nations** adopted the **ASEAN Human Rights Declaration (2012)** — criticised for subordinating rights to 'national and regional contexts', public morality and duties — with the **ASEAN Intergovernmental Commission on Human Rights (2009)** as a consultative body without a complaints function. **Asia has no regional system** because of its size and diversity (no 'Asian' identity comparable to Europe's Council or the OAS), the strength of sovereignty and non-interference in the region's politics (China, the ASEAN 'way'), the 'Asian values' position of the 1990s, the absence of a regional organisation covering the continent (SAARC's 2004 Social Charter is aspirational; the SAARC conventions on trafficking and child welfare have no monitoring), and the preference of India and others for the UN system and national institutions (the Asia-Pacific Forum of National Human Rights Institutions, 1996, is the region's substitute). The UN's regional arrangements — the OHCHR's regional offices, the Asia-Pacific Workshops since 1990 — and the Council's UPR fill the gap."));
c.push(H3("Comparison"));
c.push(TBL([
["Feature","European","Inter-American","African"],
["Treaty and date","ECHR 1950 (in force 1953)","American Convention 1969 (1978); Declaration 1948","African Charter 1981 (1986)"],
["Rights covered","Civil and political (Social Charter separate)","Civil and political; ESC by Protocol of San Salvador","All three generations plus duties"],
["Who may complain","Any person, NGO or group claiming to be a victim, directly to the Court","Any person or NGO to the Commission (no victim requirement); Court via Commission or State","Individuals/NGOs to the Commission; to the Court only against States with the Art 34(6) declaration"],
["Organs","Single permanent Court; Committee of Ministers supervises","Commission (Washington) and Court (San José)","Commission (Banjul) and Court (Arusha)"],
["Judgments","Binding, declaratory plus just satisfaction; State chooses means","Binding, detailed reparations; Court supervises compliance","Binding; AU Executive Council supervises; low compliance"],
["Derogation","Art 15","Art 27 (judicial guarantees non-derogable)","None (Commission: no derogation)"],
["Distinctive doctrines","Margin of appreciation, living instrument, positive obligations","Due diligence, amnesty invalidity, conventionality control, wide advisory jurisdiction","Claw-back clauses read down, peoples' rights, right to development, environment"],
["Weakness","Docket; non-execution (Hirst); Russia's exit","US/Canada outside Court; denunciations; funding","Political shielding; withdrawals of declarations; compliance"]
],[1800,2400,2400,2400]));
c.push(CASE("Velásquez Rodríguez v. Honduras, IACtHR 1988","Forced disappearance is a continuing, multiple violation; the State must prevent, investigate, punish and compensate; responsibility for failure of due diligence even where the perpetrator is unidentified; the burden shifts where the State controls the evidence."));
c.push(CASE("Barrios Altos v. Peru, IACtHR 2001","Self-amnesty laws for grave violations are incompatible with the Convention and lack legal effect; the obligation to investigate and punish cannot be extinguished."));
c.push(CASE("Almonacid Arellano v. Chile, IACtHR 2006","Conventionality control — domestic judges must apply the Convention and the Court's interpretations and disapply contrary national law, including Pinochet's amnesty decree."));
c.push(CASE("Social and Economic Rights Action Centre (SERAC) v. Nigeria, ACHPR 2001 (the Ogoni case)","The State's participation in and failure to control oil pollution violated the rights to health and a satisfactory environment (Arts 16, 24), the implied rights to housing and food, and the peoples' right to natural resources (Art 21); the respect-protect-promote-fulfil obligations apply to all rights."));
c.push(CASE("Centre for Minority Rights Development (Endorois) v. Kenya, ACHPR 2009","Eviction of the Endorois from Lake Bogoria violated their rights to religion, property, culture, natural resources and development; the first finding on Art 22; restitution and consultation ordered."));
c.push(CASE("African Commission v. Kenya (Ogiek), ACtHPR 2017","The Ogiek are an indigenous people; their eviction from the Mau Forest violated Arts 1, 2, 8, 14, 17, 21 and 22; the Court's first indigenous-rights judgment; reparations in 2022."));
c.push(DIG("The Inter-American Court's compliance hearings and judgments are broadcast online and its jurisprudence indexed in the 'Digesto'; the African Commission's decisions are on its website and the African Court's on its case-law portal; the Cotton Field and Sarayaku judgments used satellite imagery and forensic databases; and the ASEAN Commission's 2012 Declaration is the text against which Southeast Asian digital-rights litigation (Thailand's lèse-majesté cases, Myanmar's internet shutdowns) is measured in the absence of a court."));
c.push(TIP("Part A: 'American Convention on Human Rights', 'Inter-American Commission', 'Velásquez Rodríguez', 'Due diligence', 'Conventionality control', 'African Charter — peoples' rights', 'Duties under the African Charter', 'Claw-back clauses', 'African Court', 'SERAC v. Nigeria', 'Why no Asian regional system'. Part B: 'Describe the Inter-American system for the protection of human rights and its distinctive contributions'; or 'Examine the salient features of the African Charter on Human and Peoples' Rights and the machinery for its implementation'; or 'Compare the European, Inter-American and African systems' — the table. Part C: a person is abducted by unidentified men in a State where the army is known to disappear dissidents — Velásquez Rodríguez, due diligence, continuing violation; a State passes an amnesty for its former junta — Barrios Altos, no legal effect; a community is evicted from ancestral land for a game reserve — Endorois, Ogiek, Awas Tingni; a State party to the African Charter suspends rights in an emergency — no derogation clause, Chad case; a Southeast Asian journalist seeks a regional remedy — none; the UN Special Procedures and the Human Rights Committee (if the State is a Protocol party)."));
c.push(PB());

module.exports=c;
